# Contributing to {{ project_name }}

There are two ways to make a change here: by hand, or with the Claude Code agent kit in
`.claude/`. Both end the same way: a branch, the same checks, a pull request into
`develop` that follows the template, and a person who reviews and merges it. Agents never
merge.

- [Rules for everyone](#rules-for-everyone)
- [Contributing by hand](#contributing-by-hand)
- [Contributing with agents](#contributing-with-agents): [setup](#one-time-setup),
  [the pipeline](#the-pipeline), [small changes](#small-changes),
  [/offshore](#offshore-unattended-runs), [the teams](#the-teams),
  [what people own](#what-people-own)
- [Changing the agent kit](#changing-the-agent-kit)
- [Troubleshooting](#troubleshooting)

## Rules for everyone

- **Branches.** Branch from `develop`: `feature/<name>` (for a plan, `feature/<plan-name>`),
  `fix/<name>`, `chore/<name>`. PRs go into `develop`; `staging` and `main` only receive
  promotions. Nobody pushes to `develop`, `staging`, or `main` directly.
- **Commits.** [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/): `feat:`, `fix:`,
  `refactor:`, `test:`, `docs:`, `chore:`.
- **Conventions.** [CLAUDE.md](../CLAUDE.md) and each area's `Agents.md` are the rules for
  people and agents alike. When a plan or a prompt disagrees with them, they win.
- **Done means verified.** Run the checks for what you touched
  ([CLAUDE.md](../CLAUDE.md#done-means-verified)) and report real results. CI runs the
  same checks; the `PR check` job must be green before merging.
- **Stories.** A PR that finishes a story says so in its Closes section:
{%- if tracker == "linear" %}
  `Closes {{ tracker_key }}-12` (or `Refs {{ tracker_key }}-12` for partial work).
{%- elif tracker == "github" %}
  `Closes #12` (or `Refs #12` for partial work).
{%- else %}
  `Closes <plan>#S3` (or `Refs <plan>#S3` for partial work).
{%- endif %}
- **Decisions.** Something decided on purpose has a record in
  [decisions/](decisions/README.md). Reversing one takes a new record, not just a PR.

## Contributing by hand

1. Set up once with [First-time setup](dev_guides/onboarding/1_FIRST_TIME_SETUP.md); the
   daily loop is in [Daily workflow](dev_guides/onboarding/2_DAILY_WORKFLOW.md).
2. For a feature, follow [NewFeatureChecklist.md](NewFeatureChecklist.md). It lists
   every layer a feature touches and the order to build them in.
3. Write tests with the change. Coverage floors only go up.
4. After an API change run `npm run gen:api` and commit both generated files.
5. Run the checks, push, and open a PR. Fill in every section of the template, including
   the three questions: reviewers use them as acceptance criteria.
6. Add a line under `## [Unreleased]` in [CHANGELOG.md](../CHANGELOG.md) for anything a
   user or operator would notice ([VERSIONING.md](VERSIONING.md)).

Multi-PR features get a plan in [.implementation_plans/](../.implementation_plans/README.md),
whether a person or an agent writes it.

## Contributing with agents

The kit is a set of skills (slash commands) that plan, build, review, and document work,
each run by a small team of specialist agents. It encodes this repository's conventions,
so a new contributor's agent works the way the rest of the team does.

### What's in `.claude/`

| Path | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| `reference/project.md` | Project facts every skill reads: workspaces, check commands, doc locations, review specialists, personas. |
| `reference/tracker.md` | How skills talk to the issue tracker: seven numbered operations, the story format, and ID rules. |
| `skills/<name>/SKILL.md` | The slash commands (`/plan-product`, `/build`, ...). Each one orchestrates a team. |
| `agents/<name>.md` | Registered subagents. Each runs in a fresh context with its own model and a restricted tool list. |
| `roles/<skill>/<role>.md` | Personas a skill adopts inline (Architect, Coder, Critic, ...). No frontmatter, no separate context. |
| `hooks/guardrails.sh` | Safety net for unattended runs (see [/offshore](#offshore-unattended-runs)). |
| `settings.json` | Wires the hook and pre-approves the check commands so agents don't prompt for them. |

`CLAUDE.md` and the `Agents.md` guides sit above all of this. The kit points at them rather
than copying them, so fixing a convention there fixes it for every agent.

### One-time setup

1. **Install [Claude Code](https://code.claude.com/docs)** and open it in the repository.
   Accept the workspace trust prompt; until you do, `.claude/settings.json` permissions are
   ignored and every check command asks first.
2. **Install `jq`.** The guardrail hook needs it to read tool calls; without it the hook
   allows everything.
3. **Connect the tracker.**
{%- if tracker == "linear" %}
   Stories live in Linear, team `{{ tracker_key }}`. `.mcp.json` configures Linear's MCP
   server; run `/mcp` in Claude Code and sign in once. Then create the labels stories use
   (Linear → Settings → Labels): one per persona (`admin`, `user`, `visitor`, `developer`,
   or your own from `.claude/reference/project.md`) and `uncertainty/low`,
   `uncertainty/med`, `uncertainty/high`. Skills never invent labels; they tell you
   which one is missing. Connect Linear's GitHub integration so `Closes {{ tracker_key }}-12`
   moves the issue when the PR merges.
{%- elif tracker == "github" %}
   Stories are GitHub issues in `{{ github_repository }}`. The agent needs the GitHub MCP
   tools or an authenticated `gh` (`gh auth login`). Create the labels once:

   ```bash
   for l in epic story persona:admin persona:user persona:visitor persona:developer \
     priority:must priority:should priority:could size:S size:M size:L \
     uncertainty:low uncertainty:med uncertainty:high; do
     gh label create "$l" --repo {{ github_repository }} 2>/dev/null || true
   done
   ```

   Stories are attached to their epic as sub-issues; `Closes #12` closes an issue when the
   PR merges.
{%- else %}
   There is no external tracker: each plan keeps its stories in
   `.implementation_plans/<plan>/STORIES.md` (IDs `S1`, `S2`, ...), committed with the
   plan. Nothing to connect.
{%- endif %}

   To switch trackers later, change `tracker` in `.copier-answers.yml` and run
   `uvx copier update --trust`; the skills only ever go through `.claude/reference/tracker.md`,
   which is regenerated.
4. **Make the personas yours.** Replace the examples in the Personas table of
   `.claude/reference/project.md`. Every story names one.
5. **Docker** for integration tests, and Chromium for story tests
   (`npx playwright install chromium`), as for manual work.
{%- if include_claude_review %}
6. **Automated PR review.** `.github/workflows/claude-review.yml` reviews every PR push.
   Add the repository secret `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` (create one with
   `claude setup-token`).
{%- endif %}

### The pipeline

```
/plan-product → /plan-tech                      what to build, then how, in phases
→ for each phase: /prep → /build → /review      one reviewable change at a time
→ /write-docs → push and open the PR → /address-pr-comments
```

| Step | You type | It reads | It produces | You check |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Product plan | `/plan-product <feature idea>` (`--epic <ref> <use case>` to extend an epic, `--review <plan>` to find uncovered flows) | Existing epics, plans, `docs/feature-map.md`, the decision records | An epic and stories with Given/When/Then criteria, **{% if tracker == "none" %}written to the plan's `STORIES.md`{% else %}created in the tracker{% endif %} only after you approve the draft** | Personas, scope, and that each story is something a user can see |
| Tech plan | `/plan-tech <epic or feature>` | The epic's stories, the codebase, the area guides | `.implementation_plans/<plan>/`: a README and one doc per phase, each phase one reviewable PR mapped to the stories it implements | Phase boundaries, the assumptions it lists, anything touching auth, data, or infrastructure |
| Prep | `/prep <plan> phase <N>` | The phase doc and the code it names | READY TO IMPLEMENT (the phase becomes `Ready`), NEEDS REVISION, or BLOCKED, with a punch list | Anything BLOCKED, or a revision you disagree with |
| Build | `/build <plan> phase <N>` (or `/build <instructions>` for ad-hoc work) | The phase doc, the prep report, the guides | Commits on your branch, all checks run, an independent review, the phase marked `Done` with `Closes`/`Refs` lines | The final report: every deliverable DONE and every check PASS, or said otherwise |
| Review | `/review` (`phase <N> of <plan>`, `against <branch>`, `just backend`, `3 cycles`, `--full`) | The branch diff, the decision records | BLOCK / WARN / NOTE findings and a verdict: APPROVED, CHANGES REQUESTED, or NEEDS DISCUSSION | That BLOCKs were fixed and WARNs were fixed or answered |
| Docs | `/write-docs <plan, phase, PR, or description>` | What changed | Updated guides, `docs/feature-map.md`, and an `[Unreleased]` changelog entry, each verified | The wording of anything user-facing |
| PR feedback | `/address-pr-comments [PR]` | Every review thread and failing check | Root-cause fixes, a reply on every thread, resolved threads, a push | That the replies say what actually changed |

`/build` doesn't push or open the PR. Push the branch and open the PR yourself (or ask the
agent to), filling in the template; the Closes section repeats the keyword lines `/build`
wrote.

**A typical feature:**

```text
/plan-product let signed-in users share a note with another user by email
  → approve or edit the drafted stories; they're created in the tracker
/plan-tech note sharing
  → read .implementation_plans/note-sharing/, adjust, commit
git switch -c feature/note-sharing
/prep note-sharing phase 1     → READY TO IMPLEMENT
/build note-sharing phase 1    → commits, checks, review, phase 1 Done
/review                        → APPROVED
... phases 2..N the same way; one PR per phase, or one at the end ...
/write-docs note-sharing
git push -u origin feature/note-sharing, open the PR
/address-pr-comments           → after reviewers and CI have had their say
```

The pipeline skills run only when you type them (or `/offshore` calls them); an ordinary
request in chat doesn't start one.

### Small changes

A bug fix, a copy change, or a one-file refactor doesn't need a plan. Ask the agent
directly, or use `/build <instructions>` to get the same Coder, Critic, Validator, and
independent-review loop without a phase doc. Run `/review` before you open the PR either
way.

When an agent is guessing or cutting corners, type `/rigor <what went wrong>`. It stops,
finds what it assumed instead of verified, and redoes the work.

### /offshore: unattended runs

`/offshore <plan, story or epic ID, or description>` runs the whole pipeline with no one
watching and leaves you a green PR into `develop`, **open and unmerged**, plus a briefing.
Use it for well-specified work you'd otherwise supervise step by step. Only a person can
start it; no skill or agent may.

```
plan-product (if needed) → plan-tech (if needed)
→ per phase: prep → build → review (1-3 rounds by diff size)
→ integration gate (the full check suite) → write-docs → MANUAL_QA.md
→ one PR into develop → fix its CI and review findings → stop, unmerged → BRIEFING.md
```

**Before you start it:** Docker running (or the integration tests are the one suite it may
skip, and it tells you), `jq` installed, GitHub access (MCP tools or `gh`), and the tracker
connected. For anything product-shaped, run `/plan-product` yourself first so you approve
the stories: an unattended run decides open questions on its own and lists them.

**What you get back**, in `.implementation_plans/<plan>/`:

| File | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| `BRIEFING.md` | What happened, every decision it made on its own, and your Action Items (always ending with "review the PR, work through `MANUAL_QA.md`, merge when ready") |
| `MANUAL_QA.md` | Steps for a person to accept the feature in a browser. The run writes them; it doesn't run them. |
| `CHECKPOINT.json` | Run state. Re-typing `/offshore <plan>` resumes from it after a crash or a stop. |

**Guardrails.** While a run is active, `.offshore/active` exists, and
`.claude/hooks/guardrails.sh` blocks pushes to `develop`/`staging`/`main`, force pushes
without `--force-with-lease`, `--no-verify`, `git reset --hard`, `git commit --amend`,
merging a PR, and questions to you. Every decision and spawn is logged in `.offshore/`.
The hook is a net under the rules, not the rules: the skill follows them whether or not the
hook would catch a slip. To stop a run, tell it "stop"; it finishes at the next safe point
and writes the briefing. If a run died and left `.offshore/active` behind, delete it.

### The teams

Each skill is an orchestrator with a team. Team members come in two kinds:

- **Subagents** (`.claude/agents/`) run in a fresh context with their own model and a
  restricted tool list, and return a structured result. The kit uses them where fresh eyes
  matter (the independent reviewer never saw the code being written) or where the work
  would flood the orchestrator's context (checking every claim in a phase doc).
- **Roles** (`.claude/roles/`) are personas the orchestrator reads and adopts inline, one
  at a time, in its own context. They're cheap and share everything the orchestrator
  knows, which suits steps that build on each other (Architect → Coder → Critic).

A subagent can't spawn subagents. When a skill runs inside one (as every step of
`/offshore` does), it reads the subagent's file and applies it inline, and says so in its
report.

| Skill | Model | Team (roles inline, **subagents** in bold) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `/plan-product` | sonnet | Overlap Analyst, **domain-expert** (fable), AC Reviewer, UX Flow Analyst |
| `/plan-tech` | fable | Lead Planner, Backend / Frontend / UI Kit Planners, **decision-checker** (opus) |
| `/prep` | sonnet | **drift-detector** (sonnet), **decision-checker** (opus), **prep-auditor** (opus) |
| `/build` | opus | Architect, UI Designer, Coder, Critic, Validator, specialist reviewers, Fixer, **decision-checker** (opus), **review-lead** (fable) |
| `/review` | sonnet | Backend / Frontend / UI Kit / Docs reviewers (picked by the paths changed), lead cross-cutting review, **decision-checker** (opus), **review-lead** (fable) for extra cycles |
| `/write-docs` | sonnet | **scribe**, **scribe-changelog**, **scribe-reviewer** (all sonnet) |
| `/address-pr-comments` | opus | none; it works the threads itself |
| `/offshore` | opus | One general-purpose subagent per unit of work, each running the skills above |

**Model tiers** follow the judgement a step needs: `fable` where a mistake is expensive and
hard to spot later (the technical plan, the independent review, domain constraints),
`opus` for building and adversarial checking, `sonnet` for orchestration, mechanical
verification, and docs. A skill's `model` and `effort` apply when you type the command; a
skill invoked by another agent runs on that agent's model. A subagent's apply every time
it's spawned.

### What people own

Agents do the typing and a lot of the checking. These stay with people:

- **What to build.** Approving stories (`/plan-product` creates nothing until you say so),
  the personas, and scope.
- **Reading the plan** before the first `/prep`, above all for auth, data retention,
  migrations, and cost.
- **Decisions the plan doesn't settle.** Skills stop and ask (an unattended run lists them
  in the briefing). Accepting a decision record is always a person's call.
- **Reviewing the PR.** The agents' review is a first pass, not an approval. Read these
  closely every time: migrations, auth and data scoping, anything under `infra/` or a
  `template.yaml` (IAM above all), new dependencies, and changes to `.claude/` or
  `.github/`.
- **Manual QA** from `MANUAL_QA.md` or the PR's three questions.
- **Merging, promoting, and deploying.** Agents never merge; promotions to `staging` and
  `main` and the `prod` environment's approval are people's.

## Changing the agent kit

The kit is code: change it in a PR, and review it like code. It came from the project
template, so a change that isn't specific to this project is worth sending upstream too;
local edits to kit files can conflict on `uvx copier update`.

**Put facts where they belong.**

| To change | Edit |
| --- | --- |
| A convention (how we name, test, validate, style) | The area's `Agents.md` or `CLAUDE.md`. Every agent reads those. |
| A workspace, check command, doc location, reviewer mapping, or persona | `.claude/reference/project.md` |
| How the tracker is used | `.claude/reference/tracker.md` (or switch trackers with Copier) |
| How a step is done, or who does it | The skill, role, or subagent file |

**Adding a skill.** Create `.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`:

```yaml
---
name: <name>
description: "What it does and when to run it. The model uses this to decide when the skill applies, so say when not to use it too."
argument-hint: "<what to pass>"
model: sonnet            # optional: the model when a person types the command
effort: high             # optional
disable-model-invocation: true   # optional: only a person can start it (as /offshore)
---
```

Keep `disable-model-invocation` off for any skill `/offshore` calls: it blocks the Skill
tool, too.

**Subagent or role?**

| Make it a subagent when | Make it a role when |
| --- | --- |
| It must judge work it didn't watch being made (review, audit) | It builds on what the orchestrator just did |
| Its input is large and its output small (a report, a verdict) | It needs the whole conversation so far |
| It needs a different model or fewer tools | The orchestrator's model and tools are fine |

A subagent is `.claude/agents/<name>.md`:

```yaml
---
name: <name>
description: "Spawned by /<skill> Step <N>. What it checks and what it returns."
tools: Read, Grep, Glob          # least privilege; reviewers never get Edit or Write
model: opus
effort: high
---
```

Then add it to the calling skill's team table and spawn step, with the inline fallback
("If the Agent tool isn't available to you, read `.claude/agents/<name>.md` and apply it
inline"). Descriptions cite step numbers; renumbering a skill means updating them.

A role is a plain Markdown file in `.claude/roles/<skill>/`: personality, process,
checklist, and output format. The skill reads it each time it switches to it.

**Before you merge a kit change:**

- After touching the hook: `bash .claude/hooks/test-guardrails.sh`.
- Check that Claude Code still loads everything: start a new session and run `/agents`,
  or `claude -p "List the custom subagents and skills from this project"`.
- Run the changed skill on a real, small change and read what it produced.

## Troubleshooting

| Symptom | Cause and fix |
| --- | --- |
| Commands are blocked with `OFFSHORE GUARDRAIL` but no run is going | A run died and left `.offshore/active`. Delete it. |
| Every check command asks for permission | The workspace isn't trusted yet, so `.claude/settings.json` is ignored. Restart Claude Code here and accept the prompt. |
{%- if tracker != "none" %}
| A skill reports `SKIPPED — tracker unavailable` | The tracker couldn't be reached after retries.{% if tracker == "linear" %} Re-authenticate with `/mcp`.{% elif tracker == "github" %} Check `gh auth status` or the GitHub connection.{% endif %} Re-run the step; skills search before they create, so nothing is duplicated. |
| A skill says a label is missing | Create it in the tracker; skills don't invent labels. |
{%- endif %}
| Story tests fail to launch a browser | `npx playwright install chromium`, or set `CHROMIUM_EXECUTABLE_PATH`. |
| An agent contradicts a guide | The guide wins. Point the agent at it, and if the kit caused it, fix the kit file. |
